anchor:  PERFORATION OF
anchor:  INITIAL FRAGMENT VELOCITY 3,120 F/S
anchor:  # 75-MM H.E. SHELL, M48

# VERIFIED AGAINST THE PAGE IMAGE, 2026-08-02. All 50 cells were read off a
# 200-dpi render of source.pdf p.84 and match this CSV exactly. See
# 75mm-m48-casualties.invariant for the shared provenance note.
#
# Criterion tabulated: PERFORATION OF 1/8-IN. MILD STEEL. Same five columns as
# the casualties table it is interleaved with (see 75mm-m48-casualties.invariant).
#
# ADMISSIBILITY NOTE -- this table has NO energy closure. The source states no
# numeric perforation threshold, and 1/2 m v^2 on these rows runs 272-829 ft-lb,
# rising with range, because defeating a fixed plate is not a fixed-energy
# criterion. Identity was ORIGINALLY established by ELIMINATION: the page
# carries exactly two interleaved tables, the other closes on 58 ft-lb, so this
# is the remaining one. That argument is only sound while both halves of the
# pair are transcribed together -- do not split them.
#
# It is no longer the only argument. The retained scan gives this table a
# DIRECT, positive identity check that needs no closure of its own: the caption
# "TABLE 39 / PERFORATION OF 1/8 IN. MILD STEEL" is printed above it in the
# right half of the page, and every discriminating value in this CSV is
# physically printed in that half. That is what
# checks/ordnance-1944-page-geometry.py asserts, and it is the check that
# would have caught the original inversion.
#
# Consequence: the monotonicity checks below are structural sanity only. They
# CANNOT distinguish this column from the casualties column (both are monotone
# in every field), so they must never be cited as a column-identity check --
# doing exactly that is what inverted three committed scripts.
monotonic:  r_ft increasing
monotonic:  N non-increasing
monotonic:  B non-increasing
monotonic:  m_oz non-decreasing
monotonic:  v_fps non-increasing
